CAT = "caautoEngine"; SEG = "ENG"
# RSOS 2023 MWA B: Diagnoses and repairs engine and engine support systems (B-4 to B-9)
Q = [
("A dry compression test shows one low cylinder. Adding oil raises it a lot. What is the likely cause?",
 "Worn piston rings", ["A burned exhaust valve", "A leaking intake valve", "A worn camshaft lobe"],
 "Oil temporarily seals the gap between rings and cylinder wall, so a big rise points to ring or wall wear. A valve leak would stay low because oil does not seal a valve seat."),
("Two side-by-side cylinders both read low on a compression test. What should you suspect?",
 "A head gasket breach", ["Two worn ignition coils", "A clogged fuel filter", "A slipped serpentine belt"],
 "Low readings in adjacent cylinders point to a breach in the head gasket between them. Coils and fuel delivery do not affect a cranking compression test."),
("During a cylinder leakage test, air is heard at the tailpipe. Which part is leaking?",
 "The exhaust valve", ["The intake valve", "The piston rings", "The head gasket"],
 "Air escaping past the exhaust valve exits through the exhaust system. Intake valve leakage is heard at the throttle body and ring leakage at the oil filler or PCV."),
("During a cylinder leakage test, bubbles appear in the radiator filler neck. What does this point to?",
 "A failed head gasket", ["A leaking intake valve", "A stuck thermostat", "A weak radiator cap"],
 "Bubbles in the coolant mean cylinder pressure is reaching the cooling jacket. That happens through a failed head gasket or a crack in the head or block."),
("A vacuum gauge needle drops sharply at a regular interval at idle. What is the likely fault?",
 "A burned valve", ["A restricted exhaust", "Late ignition timing", "A vacuum leak at idle"],
 "A regular drop each time one cylinder's turn comes up shows a valve that is not sealing. A restricted exhaust shows as a slow fall in vacuum when engine speed is held up."),
("Engine vacuum falls slowly while the engine is held at 2500 rpm. What should you suspect?",
 "A restricted exhaust", ["Worn piston rings", "A burned exhaust valve", "A leaking PCV hose"],
 "Back pressure builds when exhaust cannot leave freely, so vacuum sinks the longer the engine is held at speed. A plugged catalytic converter is a common cause."),
("Long-term fuel trim reads +20% at idle but near 0% at 2500 rpm. What is the most likely cause?",
 "An unmetered vacuum leak", ["A leaking fuel injector", "A failed downstream O2 sensor", "A restricted exhaust"],
 "A vacuum leak adds a fixed amount of unmetered air, which matters most at idle when total airflow is small. At higher airflow it becomes a small share, so the trim settles."),
("Fuel trims are high positive at all speeds and worse under load. What should you check?",
 "Fuel pressure and volume", ["The PCV valve at idle", "The EGR valve seat", "The coolant temperature"],
 "A lean condition that grows with demand suggests the fuel system cannot keep up, such as a weak pump or restricted filter. A vacuum leak is worst at idle instead."),
("A narrowband upstream oxygen sensor stays near 0.85 V. What is the engine running?",
 "Rich", ["Lean", "At stoichiometric", "With a misfire"],
 "A zirconia sensor's voltage rises when there is little oxygen in the exhaust, which means a rich mixture. A lean mixture holds it near 0.1 V."),
("The downstream O2 sensor signal now mirrors the upstream sensor. What does this indicate?",
 "Low catalyst efficiency", ["A healthy catalyst", "A rich running engine", "A failed upstream sensor"],
 "A working catalyst stores oxygen, so the downstream sensor stays fairly steady. When it switches like the upstream sensor, the converter is no longer storing oxygen."),
("A misfire stays in cylinder 3 after its coil is swapped with cylinder 2. What is ruled out?",
 "The ignition coil", ["The spark plug", "The fuel injector", "Cylinder compression"],
 "If the coil were at fault, the misfire would have moved to cylinder 2 with it. The plug, injector and mechanical condition of cylinder 3 remain to be checked."),
("A vehicle runs rough and is hard to start right after refuelling. Which part is a common cause?",
 "A purge valve stuck open", ["A clogged air filter", "A weak fuel pump relay", "A shorted knock sensor"],
 "A purge valve stuck open lets fuel vapour and liquid from a full canister flood the intake. The engine then runs rich and stumbles right after a fill-up."),
("What does exhaust gas recirculation mainly reduce?",
 "Oxides of nitrogen", ["Carbon monoxide", "Hydrocarbons", "Carbon dioxide"],
 "Inert exhaust gas lowers peak combustion temperature, which is what forms NOx. CO and HC are handled mainly by the catalytic converter."),
("An EGR valve stuck open will most likely cause which symptom?",
 "Rough idle or stalling", ["Engine pinging under load", "High NOx at cruise", "Hard starting when hot only"],
 "Exhaust gas at idle dilutes the small air charge and the engine stumbles or stalls. An EGR valve stuck closed is what raises NOx and can cause pinging."),
("On a direct-injection gasoline engine, why do intake valves build up carbon?",
 "No fuel washes them clean", ["The valves run too hot", "The engine oil is too thin", "The injectors drip on them"],
 "Port injection sprays fuel onto the back of the valves and keeps them clean. With direct injection, PCV oil vapour bakes onto the dry valves instead."),
("A thermostat stuck open will most likely cause which complaint?",
 "Slow warm-up and weak heat", ["Overheating in slow traffic", "Coolant mixing into the oil", "A high temperature gauge"],
 "Coolant flows through the radiator all the time, so the engine takes too long to reach operating temperature and heater output stays low. A related DTC may also set."),
("What does a cooling system pressure tester mainly find?",
 "External and internal leaks", ["A sticking thermostat", "Low coolant freeze point", "A slipping water pump belt"],
 "Pressurizing the system to cap rating shows leaks at hoses, the radiator and the water pump, and a falling gauge with no visible leak points to an internal one."),
("What does a chemical combustion leak test detect in the cooling system?",
 "Combustion gas in coolant", ["Engine oil in the coolant", "Air trapped in the heater core", "Acidic coolant from age"],
 "The test fluid changes colour when combustion gases pass through it from the radiator neck. Their presence points to a head gasket or a cracked head."),
("On an interference engine, what can happen if the timing belt breaks?",
 "Valves can hit the pistons", ["The engine runs on 3 cylinders", "The oil pump stops working", "Only the water pump fails"],
 "In an interference design the open valves and the piston share space at different times. If the camshaft stops while the crank turns, they collide and bend valves."),
("A stretched timing chain on a VVT engine most likely sets which kind of code?",
 "Crank and cam correlation", ["Catalyst efficiency below limit", "EVAP system small leak", "Knock sensor circuit"],
 "Chain stretch makes the camshaft lag the crankshaft, and the PCM sees the cam signal out of its expected window. Correlation codes are a common result."),
("A VVT system sets a code after an oil change. What should be checked first?",
 "Oil level and viscosity", ["The timing belt tension", "The coolant mixture", "The spark plug gap"],
 "Cam phasers are moved by engine oil through a control solenoid. Low oil or the wrong viscosity changes how they respond and can set a VVT performance code."),
("Why is a mechanical gauge used to confirm a low oil pressure warning?",
 "The sender may be faulty", ["Gauges are required by law", "The PCM cannot read pressure", "It also measures oil quality"],
 "A warning light or dash gauge relies on the sender and circuit. A mechanical gauge in the sender port gives the true pressure before you condemn the engine."),
("A blocked PCV system will most likely cause which problem?",
 "Oil leaks at seals", ["Overcharging at idle", "Lean misfire at cruise", "Low coolant temperature"],
 "Blow-by gases build pressure in the crankcase when they cannot be drawn out. The pressure pushes oil past seals and gaskets."),
("During an injector balance test, one injector shows much less pressure drop. What is likely?",
 "It is restricted", ["It is leaking", "It is flowing too much", "It has a short circuit"],
 "Each injector is pulsed for the same time, so a smaller pressure drop means less fuel passed through. A clogged or sticking injector is the usual cause."),
("A diesel blows white smoke on a cold start that clears as it warms. What should you check first?",
 "The glow plug system", ["The turbocharger seals", "The air filter", "The DPF pressure sensor"],
 "Weak preheat leaves fuel unburned in a cold cylinder, giving white smoke until the engine warms. Blue smoke would point to oil, such as turbo seals."),
("A diesel blows black smoke under load. What is a common cause?",
 "Restricted intake air", ["Low fuel return flow", "A weak glow plug", "Coolant in the cylinder"],
 "Black smoke is unburned carbon from too much fuel for the air available. A plugged air filter, a boost leak after the turbo or low boost all cause it."),
("What does the DPF differential pressure sensor measure?",
 "Soot load in the filter", ["Exhaust temperature", "NOx after the SCR", "Turbo boost pressure"],
 "Pressure before and after the filter rises as soot collects. The PCM uses that difference to decide when a regeneration is needed."),
("What does diesel exhaust fluid do in an SCR system?",
 "Helps convert NOx", ["Burns off soot in the DPF", "Cools the EGR gases", "Lubricates the injectors"],
 "DEF, a urea solution, breaks down into ammonia in the hot exhaust. In the SCR catalyst the ammonia turns NOx into nitrogen and water."),
("Why should the DEF tank never be filled with diesel fuel?",
 "It damages the SCR system", ["It freezes faster in winter", "It raises NOx sensor voltage", "It thickens the fuel filter"],
 "Fuel in the DEF tank contaminates the pump, lines, injector and catalyst, and repair usually means replacing the DEF system parts. Caps and nozzles differ to prevent it."),
("What is the main hazard of loosening a fuel line on a running common-rail diesel?",
 "Fuel injected through skin", ["Fuel gelling in the lines", "Air locking the fuel pump", "A sudden loss of turbo boost"],
 "Common-rail pressure is high enough to drive fuel through skin, which is a medical emergency. Shut the engine off and let pressure bleed down as the maker specifies."),
("A diesel fuel filter often includes a water separator. Why is water a concern?",
 "It damages pumps and injectors", ["It raises the cetane number", "It plugs the air filter", "It lowers exhaust temperature"],
 "High-pressure pumps and injectors rely on diesel for lubrication and are built to tight tolerances. Water causes wear and corrosion, so the separator must be drained."),
("What does a variable geometry turbocharger change to control boost?",
 "Exhaust flow to the turbine", ["Oil flow to the shaft bearings", "Intake camshaft timing", "Common rail fuel pressure"],
 "Movable vanes change the angle and speed of exhaust gas hitting the turbine. Closing them builds boost quickly at low engine speed."),
("What is a forced, or parked, DPF regeneration?",
 "A scan tool commanded burn-off", ["A DPF cleaning with water", "Replacing the DPF filter", "Resetting the soot counter only"],
 "When driving has not cleared the filter, a scan tool can command the engine to raise exhaust temperature while parked and burn off the soot. Keep the area clear of anything that can burn."),
("Which diesel fuel property describes how readily it ignites under compression?",
 "Cetane number", ["Octane rating", "Cloud point", "Flash point"],
 "Cetane measures ignition quality, so higher cetane means a shorter ignition delay. Octane is the gasoline measure of resistance to knock."),
("Why is a wideband air-fuel sensor used instead of a narrowband sensor on many engines?",
 "It reads the actual ratio", ["It needs no heater circuit", "It works only when cold", "It is cheaper to replace"],
 "A narrowband sensor only shows rich or lean around stoichiometric. A wideband sensor uses a pumping current to report the actual ratio across a wide range."),
("A MAF sensor under-reports airflow. How does the engine usually respond?",
 "It runs lean", ["It runs rich", "It overheats", "It loses compression"],
 "The PCM meters fuel from the measured airflow, so less reported air means too little fuel. Positive fuel trims climb as the oxygen sensor sees the lean mixture."),
]
